^SAMPLE = GSM9519394
!Sample_title = BC01
!Sample_geo_accession = GSM9519394
!Sample_status = Public on May 05 2026
!Sample_submission_date = Feb 13 2026
!Sample_last_update_date = May 05 2026
!Sample_type = SRA
!Sample_channel_count = 1
!Sample_source_name_ch1 = Bladder
!Sample_organism_ch1 = Homo sapiens
!Sample_taxid_ch1 = 9606
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = tissue: Bladder
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = cell type: primary muscle-invasive bladder carcinoma
!Sample_molecule_ch1 = total RNA
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = FFPE tissue blocks were sectioned at 5-μm thickness using a microtome and stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) following standard pathology protocols. All slides underwent independent review by three dedicated genitourinary pathologists (Rossana, Ziba and Diana) to confirm urothelial carcinoma histology, assess tumor cellularity, exclude necrotic or artifact-prone regions, and delineate tumor-enriched areas suitable for spatial transcriptomics
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = Spatial transcriptomics (ST) analysis on FFPE slides were performed with the Visium spatial technology from 10x Genomics. Two to three consecutive tissue sections of 5-μm thickness were collected for RNA extraction with the Qiagen RNeasy FFPE Kit to assess the RNA quality of the tissue. The purified RNA was immediately processed to calculate the percentage of total RNA fragments >200 nucleotides (DV200) using the Agilent 4200 TapeStation system and High Sensitivity RNA ScreenTape Analysis (Agilent Technologies). Based on DV200 evaluation, blocks with DV200 >30% were selected for proceeding with sectioning. The area of interest (11 x 11 mm) on section was carefully placed within the allowable area to ensure compatibility with the Visium CytAssist instrument. The tissues were then deparaffinized, stained, and decross-linked, followed by probe hybridization, ligation, CytAssist enabled RNA digestion and oligo capture, release, and extension. The Visium spatial gene expression FFPE libraries were constructed using the Visium CytAssist Spatial Gene Expression for FFPE Human Transcriptome Probe Kit (PN-1000444) following the manufacturer's guidance. Constructed libraries were sequenced on the Illumina NovaSeq 6000 platforms to achieve a depth of at least 75,000 mean read pairs per spot. H&E images with fiducial frames were acquired at 40× resolution using a high-definition digital pathology scanner (Vectra Polaris, Akoya Biosciences) and registered to the capture areas.
!Sample_description = Library name: BC01
!Sample_data_processing = The demultiplexed FASTQ files were processed using Space Ranger (v2.1.0, 10x Genomics) and aligned to the GRCh38 human reference genome with default settings. The resulting UMI count matrices were imported into R (v4.3.1) and analyzed using Seurat (v5.1.0)1. Stringent quality control was performed to ensure the retention of high-confidence spatial transcriptomic (ST) profiles, whereby spots with low transcript complexity (UMI count < 200) were excluded from downstream analysis. Spots located in acellular regions such as necrosis or tissue detachment zones were manually excluded based on H&E and image alignment. Following these criteria, a total of 70,575 spots from 22 pre-treatment tumors passed quality control and were included in the final analysis, of which 23,296 were histologically annotated as malignant epithelial regions. Gene expression data were normalized using SCTransform, and batch effects across samples were assessed and mitigated using Harmony integration2. All downstream analyses, including clustering, visualization, and spatial mapping, were performed on the filtered and normalized dataset.
!Sample_data_processing = Assembly: GRCh38
!Sample_data_processing = Supplementary files format and content: Processed data files consist of filtered gene expression matrices in HDF5 format (filtered_feature_bc_matrix.h5) generated by Space Ranger (10x Genomics). The matrices contain spot-level UMI counts for all detected genes. Spatial metadata files including tissue_positions.csv, scalefactors_json.json, and corresponding high- and low-resolution histology images were also provided to enable spatial reconstruction and visualization.
!Sample_data_processing = Library strategy: Spatial Transcriptomics
!Sample_platform_id = GPL24676
